Sure! Here are five examples of using printf specifiers.

```cpp
    char const* my_string = foobar; 
    // print a signed integer.
    printf(Test %d\n, 5);
    // print a string alongside it's address.
    printf(There is a string living in %p, it says \%s\\n, my_string, my_string);
    // print a signed integer as unsigned.
    printf(The number %u is printed incorrectly.\n, -10);
    // print a % character and a floating point number.
    printf(We can use %%f to print a floating point, such as %f.\n, 10.5);
    // print using the hexadecimal representation.
    printf(The number in hex is 0x%X, 57005);
```

Below is an example of a basic smart contract for selling an electric razor scooter written in Solidity:

```solidity
pragma solidity ^0.5.16;

contract ElectricScooterSale {

address public seller;

address public buyer;

uint256 public price;

bool public sold;

event SaleConfirmed(address indexed buyer, uint256 price);

constructor(address _seller, uint256 _price) public {

seller = _seller;
price = _price;
sold = false;
}

function buyScooter() public payable {
require(!sold);
require(msg.value >= price);

buyer = msg.sender;
sold = true;
seller.transfer(msg.value);
emit SaleConfirmed(msg.sender, price);
}

}
```

In Rust, String and &str are two different types that represent strings of characters.

String is an owned string type, stored in the heap and mutable. It is dynamically resizeable, allowing you to append or remove characters after creation. String is allocated on the heap and has a longer lifetime than stack-allocated strings. Here's an example of creating a String:

let my_string = String::from(Hello, World!);

&str is a string slice, a reference to a string stored elsewhere such as in a String or string literal. It is stored in the stack, making it faster to access than a String, but it is also immutable and cannot be modified. Here's an example of creating an &str:

let my_str: &str = Hello, World!;

In general, use &str when you don't need to modify the string and String when you do. &str is faster and has a lower memory overhead than String, but it is also more restrictive as it is immutable.
